1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,072
The night of July 13th, 1953. A mountain

2
00:00:04,092 --> 00:00:07,486
front south of Kumsong, in central Korea.

3
00:00:07,574 --> 00:00:09,945
As darkness fell, the entire ridgeline

4
00:00:09,965 --> 00:00:12,303
to the north erupted in fire.

5
00:00:12,683 --> 00:00:15,312
Before the guns even slackened, Chinese

6
00:00:15,332 --> 00:00:17,702
troops came pouring down every valley.

7
00:00:17,824 --> 00:00:19,500
By midnight, in some places,

8
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:21,697
they were already a thousand meters

9
00:00:21,717 --> 00:00:24,093
inside the South Korean lines.

10
00:00:24,629 --> 00:00:26,058
The armistice would be signed

11
00:00:26,078 --> 00:00:27,901
in just fourteen days.

12
00:00:27,955 --> 00:00:30,258
And with two weeks left, the last great

13
00:00:30,278 --> 00:00:32,836
offensive of the Korean War had begun.

14
00:00:33,209 --> 00:00:34,934
The Battle of Kumsong.

15
00:00:35,021 --> 00:00:36,867
This is the story of the war's final

16
00:00:36,887 --> 00:00:38,507
battle, fought over the last

17
00:00:38,527 --> 00:00:40,861
stretch of today's armistice line.

18
00:00:41,233 --> 00:00:43,687
By June 1953, the truce talks

19
00:00:43,707 --> 00:00:46,089
at Panmunjom were nearly done.

20
00:00:46,175 --> 00:00:48,534
After two years of deadlock over prisoners,

21
00:00:48,554 --> 00:00:50,980
both sides finally agreed.

22
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,827
On June 8th, the prisoner exchange deal

23
00:00:53,847 --> 00:00:57,318
was signed. Peace seemed days away.

24
00:00:57,695 --> 00:00:59,428
But South Korean President Syngman

25
00:00:59,448 --> 00:01:01,295
Rhee bitterly opposed any

26
00:01:01,315 --> 00:01:03,491
truce that left Korea divided.

27
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:06,390
On June 18th, he suddenly set free some

28
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:08,859
twenty-seven thousand anti-communist

29
00:01:08,879 --> 00:01:11,920
prisoners who had refused repatriation.

30
00:01:12,305 --> 00:01:14,090
The communists were furious.

31
00:01:14,145 --> 00:01:15,924
And before signing any truce,

32
00:01:15,944 --> 00:01:17,654
they resolved to teach the South

33
00:01:17,674 --> 00:01:19,873
Korean army a hard lesson.

34
00:01:19,998 --> 00:01:22,220
General Mark Clark believed a main aim

35
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:25,575
was to give the ROK Army a bloody nose.

36
00:01:25,946 --> 00:01:28,131
They chose a bulge in the central front,

37
00:01:28,151 --> 00:01:31,135
pushing north toward the town of Kumsong.

38
00:01:31,265 --> 00:01:33,707
It was called the Kumsong salient.

39
00:01:34,088 --> 00:01:36,396
It was held by the ROK Capital Division

40
00:01:36,416 --> 00:01:38,945
and the divisions of ROK II Corps.

41
00:01:39,112 --> 00:01:40,856
Open to the enemy on three sides,

42
00:01:40,876 --> 00:01:42,686
it favored the attacker.

43
00:01:43,072 --> 00:01:45,549
ROK II Corps was commanded by

44
00:01:45,569 --> 00:01:48,076
Lieutenant General Chung Il-kwon.

45
00:01:48,174 --> 00:01:49,542
His corps had already been pushed

46
00:01:49,562 --> 00:01:50,840
back once in June,

47
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,224
and a far bigger storm was gathering.

48
00:01:53,596 --> 00:01:56,937
To the north, five Chinese armies, some two

49
00:01:56,957 --> 00:02:00,044
hundred forty thousand men, were massing.

50
00:02:00,154 --> 00:02:01,443
Behind them stood about

51
00:02:01,463 --> 00:02:03,786
thirteen hundred sixty guns.

52
00:02:03,925 --> 00:02:05,260
After the prisoner release,

53
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:08,004
Mao reportedly ordered a heavier blow.

54
00:02:08,386 --> 00:02:10,275
In men and in firepower,

55
00:02:10,295 --> 00:02:12,858
this time the enemy held the advantage.

56
00:02:12,997 --> 00:02:15,539
It was a final gamble to draw the line even

57
00:02:15,559 --> 00:02:18,359
one meter farther south before the truce.

58
00:02:18,738 --> 00:02:21,178
On July 13th, as the sun went down,

59
00:02:21,198 --> 00:02:24,410
the communist guns opened fire all at once.

60
00:02:24,499 --> 00:02:26,232
In moments, the South Korean

61
00:02:26,252 --> 00:02:29,031
positions were engulfed in flames.

62
00:02:29,432 --> 00:02:31,945
From July 11th, in ten days,

63
00:02:31,965 --> 00:02:34,290
the communists fired about one hundred

64
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:36,315
ninety-seven thousand shells.

65
00:02:36,405 --> 00:02:37,773
It was the heaviest ten-day

66
00:02:37,793 --> 00:02:39,553
bombardment of the war.

67
00:02:39,919 --> 00:02:41,933
Then the Chinese drove like wedges

68
00:02:41,953 --> 00:02:43,958
into both shoulders of the salient.

69
00:02:44,127 --> 00:02:46,315
The Capital Division in the west and the

70
00:02:46,335 --> 00:02:49,719
ROK 3rd Division in the east broke in turn.

71
00:02:50,112 --> 00:02:52,033
In the dark, the enemy slipped behind

72
00:02:52,053 --> 00:02:54,536
the lines and struck command posts.

73
00:02:54,705 --> 00:02:56,609
Colonel Yuk Geun-su of the Capital

74
00:02:56,629 --> 00:02:58,475
Division was killed trying to

75
00:02:58,495 --> 00:03:00,959
rescue his surrounded 1st Regiment.

76
00:03:01,350 --> 00:03:04,475
In the center, four enemy battalions surrounded

77
00:03:04,495 --> 00:03:07,490
a battalion of the ROK 6th Division.

78
00:03:07,629 --> 00:03:09,785
Phone lines were cut. No one

79
00:03:09,805 --> 00:03:12,619
knew which positions still held.

80
00:03:13,004 --> 00:03:15,959
On July 14th, Eighth Army commander General

81
00:03:15,979 --> 00:03:18,475
Maxwell Taylor made a hard decision.

82
00:03:18,632 --> 00:03:21,127
Give up the salient, fall back south of

83
00:03:21,147 --> 00:03:24,111
the Kumsong River, and rebuild the line.

84
00:03:24,502 --> 00:03:26,364
But in the rain and confusion,

85
00:03:26,384 --> 00:03:29,027
the withdrawal began to fall apart.

86
00:03:29,163 --> 00:03:30,748
Some units retreated across the

87
00:03:30,768 --> 00:03:32,421
river and far beyond the line

88
00:03:32,441 --> 00:03:34,441
they had been ordered to hold.

89
00:03:34,833 --> 00:03:36,460
The enemy's next objective lay

90
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:39,295
south: the Hwachon Reservoir.

91
00:03:39,342 --> 00:03:41,405
If it fell, even the Hwachon

92
00:03:41,425 --> 00:03:43,408
power plant that sent electricity

93
00:03:43,428 --> 00:03:45,311
to Seoul would be in danger.

94
00:03:45,692 --> 00:03:46,896
Behind the broken front,

95
00:03:46,916 --> 00:03:48,900
the South Koreans stitched together a

96
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:51,210
new line along the high ridges.

97
00:03:51,310 --> 00:03:53,634
It ran from Chokkun-san in the west,

98
00:03:53,654 --> 00:03:57,388
through Chupa Pass, to Paegam-san in the east.

99
00:03:57,769 --> 00:03:59,611
Chokkun-san rises one thousand

100
00:03:59,631 --> 00:04:01,922
seventy-three meters. Paegam-san,

101
00:04:01,942 --> 00:04:04,516
one thousand one hundred seventy-nine.

102
00:04:04,685 --> 00:04:07,112
Lose these peaks, and there were no high

103
00:04:07,132 --> 00:04:09,891
mountains left between the enemy and Hwachon.

104
00:04:10,283 --> 00:04:11,902
General Chung brought up the reserve

105
00:04:11,922 --> 00:04:13,712
11th Division and pulled the

106
00:04:13,732 --> 00:04:16,260
battered 6th and 3rd back to regroup.

107
00:04:16,341 --> 00:04:18,700
In the west, the US 3rd Division and

108
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:22,913
the airlifted 187th Airborne rushed in.

109
00:04:23,295 --> 00:04:25,495
Meanwhile, torrential rain forced the

110
00:04:25,515 --> 00:04:27,953
Chinese to halt and catch their breath.

111
00:04:28,014 --> 00:04:29,394
It gave the South Koreans

112
00:04:29,414 --> 00:04:31,241
one chance to strike back.

113
00:04:31,621 --> 00:04:34,340
On July 16th, ROK II Corps

114
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:36,173
counterattacked with the 11th,

115
00:04:36,193 --> 00:04:38,586
8th, and 5th Divisions abreast.

116
00:04:38,684 --> 00:04:40,946
Their objective was simple: retake the

117
00:04:40,966 --> 00:04:43,518
high ground south of the Kumsong River.

118
00:04:43,910 --> 00:04:45,919
Climbing slopes slick with mud,

119
00:04:45,939 --> 00:04:48,889
the soldiers took back the hills one by one.

120
00:04:49,064 --> 00:04:51,002
Night after night, the enemy struck

121
00:04:51,022 --> 00:04:53,410
back hard to keep what it had won.

122
00:04:53,774 --> 00:04:55,282
Among the fiercest fights was

123
00:04:55,302 --> 00:04:58,104
Paegam-san, lost to the Chinese.

124
00:04:58,159 --> 00:04:59,642
With the 5th Division stopped,

125
00:04:59,662 --> 00:05:02,385
the attached 7th Regiment swung in from

126
00:05:02,405 --> 00:05:04,445
the north and retook the summit.

127
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:07,679
Around Paegam-san alone, some five hundred

128
00:05:07,699 --> 00:05:10,938
seventy South Koreans were killed or missing.

129
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:13,762
On July 19th, the South Koreans

130
00:05:13,782 --> 00:05:15,423
finally stood on the high ground

131
00:05:15,443 --> 00:05:17,506
south of the Kumsong River.

132
00:05:17,573 --> 00:05:19,237
But efforts to retake the old

133
00:05:19,257 --> 00:05:21,104
positions across the river had

134
00:05:21,124 --> 00:05:23,687
to be abandoned after the 20th.

135
00:05:24,055 --> 00:05:25,710
For the final week of the war,

136
00:05:25,730 --> 00:05:29,375
ROK II Corps held this Kumsong River line.

137
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:31,890
And on July 27th, the armistice

138
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:34,027
was signed at Panmunjom.

139
00:05:34,419 --> 00:05:35,565
At ten o'clock that night,

140
00:05:35,585 --> 00:05:37,886
the guns at last fell silent.

141
00:05:37,983 --> 00:05:39,613
The armistice line was drawn

142
00:05:39,633 --> 00:05:41,377
along the very ground where the

143
00:05:41,397 --> 00:05:43,532
two sides stood at that moment.

144
00:05:43,917 --> 00:05:46,526
The ridgeline south of the Kumsong River,

145
00:05:46,546 --> 00:05:48,996
retaken in blood, became this

146
00:05:49,016 --> 00:05:51,534
stretch of today's armistice line.

147
00:05:51,653 --> 00:05:53,318
Had it lain just a few kilometers

148
00:05:53,338 --> 00:05:55,799
farther south, the map of Hwachon

149
00:05:55,819 --> 00:05:57,733
might look very different today.

150
00:05:58,096 --> 00:06:00,559
The salient itself was lost, about one

151
00:06:00,579 --> 00:06:02,834
hundred seventy square kilometers.

152
00:06:02,916 --> 00:06:05,297
By South Korean counts, some fourteen

153
00:06:05,317 --> 00:06:08,711
thousand ROK soldiers were killed, wounded,

154
00:06:08,731 --> 00:06:11,534
or missing. Eighth Army estimated over

155
00:06:11,554 --> 00:06:14,652
twenty-eight thousand Chinese casualties.

156
00:06:15,031 --> 00:06:17,196
So why could two hundred forty thousand

157
00:06:17,216 --> 00:06:19,665
troops never break through to Hwachon?

158
00:06:19,715 --> 00:06:21,458
Let's look at four reasons.

159
00:06:21,848 --> 00:06:23,947
First, the salient trap.

160
00:06:24,321 --> 00:06:26,343
Touching the enemy on three sides,

161
00:06:26,363 --> 00:06:28,051
the salient's shoulders were

162
00:06:28,071 --> 00:06:30,110
its weak points from the start.

163
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:31,961
Once a shoulder broke, every unit

164
00:06:31,981 --> 00:06:35,052
inside was at risk of being surrounded.

165
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:37,911
Second, the mountain defense line.

166
00:06:38,304 --> 00:06:40,295
The South Koreans did not fall back to

167
00:06:40,315 --> 00:06:42,561
open ground, but redrew their line

168
00:06:42,581 --> 00:06:45,223
on peaks over a thousand meters high.

169
00:06:45,339 --> 00:06:46,468
Holding the heights, they could

170
00:06:46,488 --> 00:06:49,253
stand firm with far fewer men.

171
00:06:49,643 --> 00:06:52,771
Third, reserves and reinforcements.

172
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:54,572
The corps pulled out its shattered

173
00:06:54,592 --> 00:06:55,608
divisions and threw the

174
00:06:55,628 --> 00:06:58,180
reserve 11th Division straight in.

175
00:06:58,303 --> 00:07:00,252
With American reinforcements arriving

176
00:07:00,272 --> 00:07:03,723
within a day or two, the gap never widened.

177
00:07:04,119 --> 00:07:06,832
Fourth, the counterattack in the rain.

178
00:07:07,222 --> 00:07:09,032
The South Koreans did not waste the

179
00:07:09,052 --> 00:07:11,548
days when the enemy paused for breath.

180
00:07:11,716 --> 00:07:13,324
When the side being pushed back

181
00:07:13,344 --> 00:07:15,259
struck first, the enemy's final

182
00:07:15,279 --> 00:07:17,387
breakthrough lost its momentum.

183
00:07:17,466 --> 00:07:19,085
After July 20th, the Chinese

184
00:07:19,105 --> 00:07:21,021
advanced no farther south.

185
00:07:21,405 --> 00:07:23,097
About a decade after the war,

186
00:07:23,117 --> 00:07:24,963
a young officer was serving as a

187
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:27,933
platoon leader near Paegam-san.

188
00:07:28,013 --> 00:07:30,724
In those mountains, he came across nameless

189
00:07:30,744 --> 00:07:33,858
graves marked by nothing but a wooden stake.

190
00:07:34,249 --> 00:07:36,930
That officer, Han Myung-hee, wrote the

191
00:07:36,950 --> 00:07:40,628
Korean art song Bimok, The Wooden Marker.

192
00:07:40,685 --> 00:07:42,258
The deep valley in that song

193
00:07:42,278 --> 00:07:44,183
was these very mountains.

194
00:07:44,725 --> 00:07:47,887
In autumn 2025, remains were recovered

195
00:07:47,907 --> 00:07:50,629
from a nameless hill in Cheorwon.

196
00:07:50,753 --> 00:07:52,349
They were Sergeant Kim Sun-sik of

197
00:07:52,369 --> 00:07:54,614
the ROK 6th Division, killed at

198
00:07:54,634 --> 00:07:57,354
Kumsong eight days before the truce.

199
00:07:57,730 --> 00:08:00,014
This May, after seventy-three years,

200
00:08:00,034 --> 00:08:02,517
he finally came home to his family.

201
00:08:02,586 --> 00:08:04,694
The line south of the Kumsong River is

202
00:08:04,714 --> 00:08:07,823
still drawn over the names of men like him.

203
00:08:08,371 --> 00:08:11,107
Fourteen days before the truce, a final

204
00:08:11,127 --> 00:08:14,111
offensive by two hundred forty thousand men.

205
00:08:14,231 --> 00:08:15,805
The line that refused to break

206
00:08:15,825 --> 00:08:18,346
before it is today's armistice line.

207
00:08:18,737 --> 00:08:20,305
If this story meant something to you,

208
00:08:20,325 --> 00:08:22,695
please subscribe and like this video.

209
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:24,429
We'll continue the record of a

210
00:08:24,449 --> 00:08:26,615
forgotten war in the next episode.
